                "quote_id": "5fbdcee710fe7e18425f4eeefe811890b3c8a03803f78b23685ad11309840679",
                "text": "Earlier in September 1988, European Commission President Jacques Delors addressed the British Trades Union Congress, advocating for a 'social Europe' with guaranteed workers' rights. Thatcher delivered this speech in Bruges to counter his vision, warning against the centralization of power in the European Economic Community. The future political and economic direction of the European Economic Community and the balance of power between member states and Brussels. The European project should remain a cooperative free-trade area of sovereign states rather than evolving into a centralized, federal super-state that dictates domestic economic and social policies. Britain has worked hard to reduce domestic state intervention and will not accept new bureaucratic controls imposed by a central European authority. National sovereignty and free-market economics must be vigorously defended against supranational collectivism, corporatism, and bureaucratic centralization. By publicly rejecting the Delors vision of a federal Europe, Thatcher aimed to rally opposition to centralization and protect her domestic neoliberal reforms from external reversal. The speech became a foundational text for modern British Euroscepticism, leading to the creation of the Bruges Group and sparking a long-term ideological shift in the Conservative Party that culminated in Brexit."
