            "evidence": [
              {
                "field": "bounded_packet_semantics",
                "quote_id": "03bf609fce2febd4136dee4722a9c5596fe61123411fad59bfcd06c3e821fc2f",
                "text": "Following her resignation as Prime Minister in 1990, Thatcher continued to champion free-market economics globally. She frequently argued that capitalism was not just economically efficient but fundamentally moral. The moral distinction between capitalist and socialist systems. Capitalism is inherently moral because it relies on voluntary cooperation and individual freedom, whereas socialism is coercive and subjugates citizens to the state. Capitalism possesses a moral quality that socialism lacks, as socialism merely increases government power over individuals. Economic freedom is inseparable from moral responsibility and true democracy. Contrasting the voluntary nature of market transactions with the coercive planning of socialist governments. Understanding this moral distinction prevents societies from surrendering their freedoms to state control."
              },
              {
                "field": "historical_context",
                "quote_id": "04cdf43ebd3729f0c4069ee808ba55613191533d2b12cff8753f55e8ad31c3c6",
                "text": "In late 1984, the UK was in the midst of the miners' strike, a major and bitter industrial dispute. Thatcher was emphasizing the need for trade unions to cooperate with management to ensure industrial prosperity rather than engaging in political strikes."
              },
              {
                "field": "bounded_packet_semantics",
                "quote_id": "093fe18b5e8a685a1ec1a38813fa2dfddce5ca84853723c13d1dfc2a6ac4a429",
                "text": "During a 1986 interview with Italian journalist Enzo Biagi, Thatcher was asked about the 'Iron Lady' nickname, which was originally coined by the Soviet press in 1976 following her staunch anti-communist speech on defense. Her reaction to being called the 'Iron Lady' and the qualities required to lead the United Kingdom. That the nickname is a compliment rather than an insult, as leading a historically significant and reliable nation inherently requires unyielding strength. A leader of a powerful, globally influential nation like Britain must possess a degree of unbending resolve or 'iron'. Effective national leadership on the world stage demands fortitude, resilience, and unwavering resolve. By embracing a potentially critical nickname, she reframes it as a vital leadership asset, aligning her personal brand with national strength. She asserts that she does not mind the title, as it reflects the necessary strength to defend freedom and maintain Britain's status as a reliable ally."
              },
              {
                "field": "mechanism",
                "quote_id": "0f81923e54293261a3cc8318d9ac5959008002c0a9e17354ae1e8ea881f28e29",
                "text": "By contrasting the coercive methods of socialism with the voluntary cooperation of free individuals, Thatcher frames state control as a moral failing."
              },
              {
                "field": "mechanism",
                "quote_id": "115f2b69f05bd8ad48114ece66c3c31d0149f85335b9a9c884a4d790d3268fa0",
                "text": "By highlighting the total ideological collapse of the adversary, Thatcher pivots to justifying the continued existence of the defensive alliance that opposed it."
              },
              {
                "field": "historical_context",
                "quote_id": "16c1d05225fe1d45d7ab950a5c54d39b70ef9453718a8678f405195d4af6969f",
                "text": "Delivered in 1996 as a memorial lecture for her ideological ally Keith Joseph, Thatcher reflected on conservative philosophy and warned against the resurgence of collectivism. The speech aimed to contrast conservative individualism with socialist state expansion under the guise of 'community'."
              }
            ],
